.TH SPEEDNS 1 "August 2026" "SpeeDNS" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
speedns \- compare recursive DNS resolver performance
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B speedns
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B speedns run
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B speedns completion
.BR bash | zsh | fish | powershell
.br
.B speedns resolvers
.br
.B speedns corpus
.br
.B speedns version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B speedns
is a read-only command-line benchmark for recursive DNS resolvers. It measures
UDP, TCP, DNS-over-HTTPS, DNS-over-TLS, and dedicated DNS-over-QUIC separately.
It does not change system DNS settings, require root privileges, or send
telemetry.
.PP
The default report compares the bundled resolver catalog using a reproducible
sample of domain names and shows a recommendation for each selected transport.
Use JSON or CSV output when another program needs the aggregate results.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B speedns
Run a benchmark. The command is also the default when no subcommand is given.
.TP
.B speedns run
Run a benchmark explicitly.
.TP
.B speedns completion SHELL
Write a completion script for SHELL, which may be bash, zsh, fish, or
PowerShell.
.TP
.B speedns resolvers
List bundled resolver ownership, policy, addresses, and supported transports.
.TP
.B speedns corpus
Show the embedded domain corpus provenance and checksum.
.TP
.B speedns version
Show version, commit, and build information.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --protocol udp,tcp,doh,dot,doq
Select the transports to benchmark.
.TP
.B --resolver NAME=URI
Add a custom resolver endpoint. Repeat the option for multiple endpoints.
.TP
.B --resolver-file PATH
Load strict, versioned resolver profiles from YAML.
.TP
.B --no-defaults
Do not include the bundled public resolver catalog. Use with
.B --resolver
or
.B --resolver-file
to benchmark only your own endpoints.
.TP
.B --family 4|6|both|auto
Select the resolver address family. The default
.B auto
uses the local up-interface address inventory without performing DNS lookups or
connection probes.
.TP
.B --domains PATH
Read one domain per line from a custom list. Blank lines and comments are
ignored.
.TP
.B --sample N
Sample N domains from the selected corpus.
.TP
.B --full
Use the complete selected domain list.
.TP
.B --cache-miss
Opt in to bounded random names below the IANA-reserved
.I example.com
zone instead of the warm-cache corpus. Cannot be combined with
.B --domains
or
.BR --full .
.TP
.B --cache-miss-sample N
Number of unique reserved-zone names generated for
.BR --cache-miss ,
maximum 20.
.TP
.B --seed N
Use a specific seed so the selected query order can be reproduced.
.TP
.B --type A,AAAA
Select DNS record types.
.TP
.B --timeout DURATION
Set the per-query and connection timeout, for example 2s.
.TP
.B --concurrency N
Set the maximum number of measured exchanges in flight per transport.
.TP
.B --format table|json|csv
Choose human-readable, JSON, or CSV output.
.TP
.B --output PATH
Write the report to a file instead of standard output.
.TP
.B --details
Include cold latency, jitter, response outcomes, and expanded diagnostics.
.TP
.B --raw
Include per-query observations in JSON output.
.TP
.B --profile-view
Show same-resolver transport comparisons with score confidence intervals.
Requires table or JSON output.
.TP
.B --redact-system
Redact local system resolver addresses and labels in reports.
.TP
.B --assert EXPRESSION
Assert a winner metric or profile condition, and exit
.B 4
if it does not hold. Repeatable. Accepts
.IR usable ,
.IR success ,
.IR median ,
.IR p95 ,
and
.I score
with
.BR >= ", " > ", " <= ", " < ", or " = ,
or
.IR winner=PROFILE-ID .
Rates are between 0 and 1; latencies accept a bare number of milliseconds or a
Go duration such as
.IR 50ms .
.TP
.B --include-system
Include resolvers discovered from the operating system configuration.
.TP
.B --no-color
Disable ANSI styling in table output.
.SH OUTPUT
Table output is written to standard output by default. JSON output has a
versioned schema and CSV output has one aggregate row per target. Table runs
write progress only to standard error: terminals receive one updating line,
while redirected standard error receives deterministic preparation,
measurement, and completion lines. JSON and CSV runs are silent on standard
error.
.PP
Preparation covers connection setup, cold probes, and warm-up queries.
Measurement counts completed DNS exchanges, including failures. Progress does
not show an ETA or resolver addresses and does not change report contents.
.PP
Redirected progress reports each phase when it starts, again after roughly
every 25 percent of the work in that phase, and once more with its final
counters before the next phase or the completion line. That bounds the log at
five lines per phase and protocol. A phase whose total is small or zero still
reports its final counters.
.PP
Transport-library diagnostics use the same standard-error channel. Interactive
progress clears and restores its status line around such diagnostics. If all
selected IPv6 endpoints fail before receiving a usable response, table output
summarizes them as an unavailable IPv6 path; partial IPv6 failures remain
visible per endpoint.
.PP
A qualified recommendation requires at least 20 scored comparable samples and
99 percent usable responses. A short run may show a provisional winner instead.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The comparison completed successfully.
.TP
.B 2
Input or configuration was invalid.
.TP
.B 3
The run finished but produced no comparable DNS results.
.TP
.B 4
A requested benchmark assertion did not hold. The ordinary report is written
before this status is returned.
.TP
.B 130
The run was interrupted.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/resolv.conf
System resolver configuration on Linux, and the fallback on macOS.
.TP
.I scutil --dns
Queried on macOS for scoped resolver configuration before the
.I /etc/resolv.conf
fallback.
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
speedns --protocol udp --sample 25 --type A --seed 42
speedns --format json --output result.json
speedns --protocol doq --sample 25 2>speedns-progress.log
speedns completion bash > speedns.bash
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR resolv.conf (5)
